                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Another bundle game, and another \"remake of an old classic\" sorta.  I don't want to say remake as if it was just the old game completely lifted and retooled for VR...but it was definitely heavily inspired by the old classic.  \n\nSo this is basically VR Centipede.  For those who don't remember the old Atari classic arcade game, it was basically one fire button, a track ball, and on screen you had a series of mushrooms.  From the top of the screen a centipede with several circular body segments would come down toward the bottom.  If it hit a mushroom, it would stop it's progress accross the row and go down one column.  \n\nIf there happened to be a whole bunch of mushrooms lined up vertically, that could mean the centipede would have a fast track to the bottom.  Oh and mushrooms took several hits to completely destroy, stayed in place between levels and got repaired between levels as well.  \n\nOccasionally a spider would appear and bounce up and down across the screen in a zig zag pattern offering bonus points to the player based on how close the spider was when you shot it.  \n\nSo this game is basically that exact thing.  Instead of being able to zip all across the screen with the trackball though, you're limited to driving this circular pod around the grassy landscape all while shooting glowing floating pixellated mushrooms and dragon headed centipede monster thingies.\n\nThe music is ok, but I could swear I've heard it before.  Nothing too great.  \n\nThe graphics have a kind of retro neon glow to it and is pixellated like someone drew all of it in SculptVR.  The grass, your gun, and the pod aren't pixellated though, just the enemies. \n\nAs you destroy the enemies they drop powerups and if you drive your pod over one, it picks it up and turns it into a crystal which can then be used to load the power into special ammo for your gun.\n\nSpeaking of that, you have to reload your gun.  You can't just keep firing forever, so when ammo runs out, you have to reach over, grab the sphere of old ammo and toss it away, then grab a new ammo sphere from the pod's console, load that into the back of the gun and then continue shooting. \n\nPicking up multiple spheres doesn't get you any stronger ammo nor does it give you multiples so it's use it or lose it time. There's a triple shot, a magnetic shot (?!), an explosive shot, and a couple of others.  The only one I paid much attention to was the triple shot as it spread out a bit and made aiming a bit easier.  Explosive shot didn't seem to take out more than one segment of the enemy at a time but maybe it just didn't look that way.\n \n\nThings I liked\n[list]\n  [*] It's centipede! I love me some centipede!\n  [*] the retro pixel aesthetic is nice.  Too pixellated and it would look like garbage, and too sharp and it would just look awkward.  This is a nice compromise\n  [*] Some of the different ammo types is kinda neat\n[/list]\n\nThings I didn't like so much\n[list]\n  [*] Having to reload the gun was an extra chore that didn't add anything to it.  I would understand needing to load special ammo but for plain shots? It's just an annoyance\n  [*] Some of that special ammo didn't add much to the experience either.  \n  [*] The game ramps up the difficulty way way too slowly.  I played for nearly half an hour and never once felt like I was in danger of losing the game.\n  [*] The background was kinda bland except for a series of LED style message boards that read \"YOUR ADVERTISEMENT HERE\".  Ugh... If they added advertisement to this game that would be an instant thumbs down.  Hopefully that's a joke.\n  [*] since all the enemies are above you, after a while your neck starts to hurt from looking up constantly.  \n[/list]\n\nSo really the worst thing about this game is that it's too easy.  Maybe you could teach a child to play this game and they would enjoy it.  It's still kinda cool to see a VR centipede game.",
